.TH "CSSMATHCLAMP.VALUE" "3JS" "August 4, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
CSSMathClamp.value \- CSSMathClamp: value property
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]value\f[B]\f[R] read\-only property of the
\f[CR]CSSMathClamp\f[R] interface returns a \f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R]
instance representing its preferred value.
.SH VALUE
A \f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
The following code creates a \f[CR]CSSMathClamp\f[R] object, then reads
its \f[CR]value\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] clamp = \f[B]new\f[R] CSSMathClamp(CSS.px(10), CSS.percent(50), CSS.px(500));

console.log(clamp.value); \f[I]// CSSUnitValue {value: 50, unit: \(dqpercent\(dq}\f[R]
console.log(clamp.value.value); \f[I]// 50\f[R]
console.log(clamp.value.unit); \f[I]// \(dqpercent\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]value\f[R] simply returns whatever \f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R] was
passed into the constructor \(em here that\(cqs a
\f[CR]CSSUnitValue\f[R], since \f[CR]CSS.percent(50)\f[R] is a
\f[CR]CSSUnitValue\f[R].
Passing a more complex expression, such as
\f[CR]CSS.percent(50).add(CSS.em(2))\f[R] (a \f[CR]CSSMathSum\f[R]),
means \f[CR]value\f[R] would return that \f[CR]CSSMathSum\f[R] instead.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]CSSMathClamp.lower\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]CSSMathClamp.upper\f[R]
